      <section>
        <div class="sec-kicker">Differentiator</div>
        <h2>Why KeronisEngine vs. general-purpose engines</h2>
        <p class="sec-desc">
          NautilusTrader is a strong general-purpose platform for Python strategies and broad broker catalogs.
          KeronisEngine is purpose-built when predictive models sit in the hot path.
        </p>
        <div class="diff-grid">
          <div class="diff-card accent">
            <h3>KeronisEngine (Predictive-Model Native)</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Eliminates feature-code drift between training and serving:</strong> C++ <code>StrategyStateMachine</code>s generate offline Parquet training panels and serve live inference with the same compiled code.</li>
              <li><strong>Sub-millisecond ONNX in-process:</strong> Score gradient boosters or neural nets directly on tick arrival without IPC or Python GIL overhead.</li>
              <li><strong>Prediction-market &amp; event-market native:</strong> Built-in Polymarket CLOB execution, multi-outcome tickers, and queue-notional backtesting—extensible to sports-betting exchanges.</li>
              <li><strong>Commodity hardware footprint:</strong> Full Binance live feeds (10k+ msg/s) + continuous predictions on a single ~$100 Linux server.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-card">
            <h3>When to pick NautilusTrader or HftBacktest</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Pick NautilusTrader</strong> if you want to write strategies in Python and want turnkey adapters across traditional equities, FX, and many crypto brokers out of the box.</li>
              <li><strong>Pick HftBacktest</strong> if your primary research depends on strict L3 order-queue-position simulation on CEX books in Python/Numba.</li>
              <li><strong>Keep your alpha private:</strong> With KeronisEngine, external strategies and state machines compile in via <code>-DKERONIS_STRATEGY_DIRS</code> without forking.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <div class="sec-kicker">System Internals</div>
        <h2>Modular C++20 components built for live production</h2>
        <div class="bento">
          <a href="/architecture/#ringbufferbook" class="card">
            <div class="card-tag">trading_engine</div>
            <h3>RingBufferBook</h3>
            <p>Flat tick-indexed array with 16-byte POD slots, occupancy bitmap + <code>__builtin_ctzll</code> depth walks, lazy mmap pages, and runtime window sizing.</p>
          </a>
          <a href="/live-and-paper/" class="card">
            <div class="card-tag">multi-market</div>
            <h3>Polymarket, Crypto &amp; Event Markets</h3>
            <p>Native Polymarket CLOB streams, EIP-712 bulk signing, multi-outcome tickers, and queue-notional backtesting—plus Hyperliquid (Rust signer), Aster, and Lighter L2.</p>
          </a>
          <a href="/live-and-paper/#paper-trading-paperexchange" class="card">
            <div class="card-tag">live_infra</div>
            <h3>PaperExchange Fill Model</h3>
            <p>Dedicated-thread paper venue: evaluates taker fills at <code>ack + latency</code>, fills resting remainders against quotes and subsequent prints, and stops fills on cancel.</p>
          </a>
          <a href="/architecture/#live-runtime-live_infra" class="card">
